    "result_text": "The full suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 3 tests, 0 failures \u2014 that's the complete test suite in this repo).\n\n## Summary\n\nI introduced address normalization applied when an owner is created, satisfying the spec.\n\n**New class \u2014 `AddressNormalizer`** (`rest/validation/AddressNormalizer.java`):\n- Follows the exact pattern of the existing `EmailNormalizer` / `TelephoneNormalizer` siblings (single responsibility, static utility).\n- Trims, collapses internal whitespace to a single space, upper-cases, and expands whole-word street abbreviations (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`) via a small lookup map. Token-based expansion avoids mangling substrings inside other words.\n- Returns `null` for `null` input so the required-field check remains responsible for rejecting missing values.\n\n**Controller \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`**:\n- One line added at the top: `ownerFieldsDto.setAddress(AddressNormalizer.normalize(ownerFieldsDto.getAddress()))`.\n- This single insertion point satisfies all three requirements at once:\n  - **Store/return normalized:** the mapper reads the normalized DTO address, so the saved and returned `address` is normalized.\n  - **Required-field check post-normalization:** `validateRequiredFields` runs *after* normalization, so `\"   \"` becomes `\"\"` and is rejected (400).\n  - **Comparisons use normalized form:** `owner.getAddress()` (fed to both the household duplicate check and `OwnerHouseholdRegistrar`) is already normalized, so household detection and the shared household id key are computed from it.\n\n**Doc fix:** updated the now-stale comment in `OwnerHouseholdUniquenessValidator` that claimed the stored address isn't normalized (the in-memory comparison is still justified by the non-normalized last name).\n\nI reused existing infrastructure (`OwnerFieldsValidator`, `HouseholdKey`) rather than duplicating logic, and kept each piece in a small single-responsibility unit consistent with the surrounding normalizers.",
